            "rank": "species"
          }
        },
        "evidence": [
          {
            "item": "#/texts/80",
            "quote": "Whereas galls of other Pemphigus species are formed from tissues of the leaf blade and/or petiole, galls of P. populiramulorum occur on twigs of the current year. Feeding on the young twigs causes swelling of the tissues that expand into a hollow globular structure that surrounds the stem mother and her offspring (Fig. 12). The exit opening is a narrow slit with slightly protruding lips. The location of galls on the woody tissues of the twig results in vacant galls frequently being retained on the tree during winter months. The point of attachment left by abscised galls is a characteristic flattened \"button\" region on the twig (Fig. 13).",
            "pdf_page": 11,
            "page": "291"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
